// The core library: `use core` gives the words of def-lang, one topic per file.
//
// A line's shape decides first which def wraps the others: fixed words that
// surround other defs (`api ... runs ... and sends ...`, `is ... ?`), or a `written`
// sentence that starts the line (`all of ... are ...`). Otherwise order matters: a def
// written earlier is applied last, so it is the outer part of a line. That is why
// `a is 2 + 3 > 4` means `a is ((2 + 3) > 4)`. See How it runs in the README.
// So the files are used loosest first.

// First the kernel's entries: the def language, the words entries are written with
// (kernel.def), and the flags, statements and modifiers written with them. They make
// no defs a line is read with, so their place doesn't change how a line is read.
use language/def
use kernel
use flag/written
use flag/regex
use statement/comment
use statement/argument
use statement/indent
use statement/error
use statement/echo
use statement/scope
use modifier/shared
use modifier/internal
use loops
use questions
use values
use lists
use joined
use logic
use comparison
use math
use text
use time
use numbers
use phrases
